1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,460
Hi everyone, and welcome to the Digital Factory Practicum at WenQuest.

2
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:13,560
This is Lecture 3, the factory hub. The grinder stopped;

3
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:16,560
what is the cheapest fix?

4
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,180
Here's what happened.

5
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:26,775
The robot plant's 20 extra joint reducers are due on Thursday.

6
00:00:27,175 --> 00:00:35,383
At ten on Monday the bottleneck grinder GRD-01 has a spindle fault and must

7
00:00:35,403 --> 00:00:37,029
wait for parts,

8
00:00:37,049 --> 00:00:38,674
about 24 hours.

9
00:00:39,074 --> 00:00:44,464
Three orders are queued: Summit's 8 units due Wednesday,

10
00:00:44,484 --> 00:00:50,454
the robot plant's 20 due Thursday, Harbor's 12 due much later.

11
00:00:50,854 --> 00:00:56,093
The supervisor says 24 hours is three shifts: three days late,

12
00:00:56,113 --> 00:00:59,994
600 dollars in penalties, expedite the repair.

13
00:01:00,394 --> 00:01:01,774
Is he right?

14
00:01:03,294 --> 00:01:05,126
Spot it, trace it.

15
00:01:06,646 --> 00:01:09,346
How does the hub know?

16
00:01:09,746 --> 00:01:10,326
Through rules.

17
00:01:10,726 --> 00:01:17,593
A rule is a judgement written as a program: if the data meet a condition,

18
00:01:17,613 --> 00:01:19,386
raise an exception.

19
00:01:19,786 --> 00:01:27,835
This stop fires P1, machine down; P5, more than 6 parts queued at a bottleneck;

20
00:01:27,855 --> 00:01:34,168
S1, an order may be late; and R3, a delay will cost a penalty.

21
00:01:34,568 --> 00:01:39,216
The rules check the plant every 20 seconds: they never forget,

22
00:01:39,236 --> 00:01:41,776
never tire and judge consistently.

23
00:01:42,176 --> 00:01:47,565
But they only say that something is wrong, not why or what to do,

24
00:01:47,585 --> 00:01:51,476
and these four exceptions are really one event.

25
00:01:51,876 --> 00:01:57,205
Thresholds need judgement too: too loose and alerts flood in,

26
00:01:57,225 --> 00:02:00,976
too tight and real trouble goes unreported.

27
00:02:02,496 --> 00:02:07,892
Everything in a plant is connected: operations queue on machines,

28
00:02:07,912 --> 00:02:13,642
belong to work orders, which serve orders, which belong to customers.

29
00:02:14,042 --> 00:02:18,302
The hub stores these links as a network and walks it two ways.

30
00:02:18,702 --> 00:02:22,322
Up to the cause: why is the order late?

31
00:02:22,722 --> 00:02:25,942
Its operation is queued on the grinder.

32
00:02:26,342 --> 00:02:27,922
Why is the grinder idle?

33
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:31,367
A spindle fault, waiting for parts.

34
00:02:31,767 --> 00:02:37,249
Down to the impact: three groups of work orders are pushed back,

35
00:02:37,269 --> 00:02:41,892
three orders finish later, and someone pays a penalty.

36
00:02:42,292 --> 00:02:49,192
Every link needs evidence: a message, a document, a rule.

37
00:02:49,592 --> 00:02:51,606
A link without evidence is a guess.

38
00:02:53,126 --> 00:03:00,986
This is Animation 3-1, with the downtime moving between 4 and 40 hours.

39
00:03:01,386 --> 00:03:06,610
Watch the colours: all three groups of work orders are pushed back,

40
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:10,914
yet at 24 hours only the robot plant's order turns red.

41
00:03:11,314 --> 00:03:18,475
Summit is first in the queue, ground right after the repair, done at Tue 15:12,

42
00:03:18,495 --> 00:03:23,292
and still ships Wednesday; Harbor's date is far away.

43
00:03:23,692 --> 00:03:29,371
From 26 hours Summit's slack runs out and it turns red too.

44
00:03:29,771 --> 00:03:35,865
The impact can't be guessed; it has to be worked out order by order.

45
00:03:37,385 --> 00:03:38,645
Work out the impact.

46
00:03:40,165 --> 00:03:44,625
The supervisor divided 24 by 9, about 2.7 days.

47
00:03:45,025 --> 00:03:45,747
What's wrong?

48
00:03:46,147 --> 00:03:51,487
The repair runs overnight, but the shop doesn't work at night anyway.

49
00:03:51,887 --> 00:03:58,163
What's really lost is only the overlap of the stop and the shifts,

50
00:03:58,183 --> 00:04:05,508
the two yellow blocks: 7 hours on Monday, 10 to 5, and 2 on Tuesday, 8 to 10,

51
00:04:05,528 --> 00:04:07,702
9 hours in all, not 24.

52
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:16,403
A 20-hour stop starting at 3 on Friday afternoon loses only Friday's 2 hours;

53
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:19,104
the weekend isn't worked.

54
00:04:20,624 --> 00:04:24,804
Lose 9 hours, and the order is 9 hours late?

55
00:04:25,204 --> 00:04:27,479
It depends on the slack.

56
00:04:27,879 --> 00:04:33,104
Without the stop the grinder does Summit's 5.2 hours,

57
00:04:33,124 --> 00:04:38,646
then the robot plant's 13, finishing at 10:12 Wednesday.

58
00:04:39,046 --> 00:04:41,606
The robot order ships Thursday,

59
00:04:41,626 --> 00:04:45,852
so its shafts must be done by the end of Wednesday;

60
00:04:45,872 --> 00:04:48,183
the regular shift ends at 5,

61
00:04:48,203 --> 00:04:52,429
and finishing that evening on overtime also counts.

62
00:04:52,829 --> 00:04:57,436
In regular hours that's 6.8 hours of slack.

63
00:04:57,456 --> 00:05:01,525
9 minus 6.8 leaves it 2.2 hours short:

64
00:05:01,545 --> 00:05:09,812
the shafts slip to Thursday morning and the order ships Friday, one day late,

65
00:05:09,832 --> 00:05:12,072
a 200-dollar penalty.

66
00:05:12,472 --> 00:05:18,252
The supervisor's three days and 600 dollars are three times the real figure.

67
00:05:19,771 --> 00:05:27,071
On screen, first the original order, then the robot order moved to the front.

68
00:05:27,471 --> 00:05:34,422
It's ground by 2 pm Wednesday, on time; but Summit is pushed back,

69
00:05:34,442 --> 00:05:42,554
done only at Thu 10:12, 2 days late, 400 dollars, more than the original 200.

70
00:05:42,954 --> 00:05:47,837
Reordering didn't remove the loss; it moved it to another customer.

71
00:05:48,237 --> 00:05:52,320
You only see that if the chain works out every order together.

72
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:56,140
Three remedies, simulated.

73
00:05:57,660 --> 00:06:01,721
To make up 2.2 hours there are three remedies,

74
00:06:01,741 --> 00:06:05,714
priced from the digital factory's parameters.

75
00:06:06,114 --> 00:06:15,917
Overtime: the grinder costs 52 dollars an hour, overtime 1.5 times that, 78,

76
00:06:15,937 --> 00:06:22,766
at most 4 hours a day; 2.2 hours cost 171.60 dollars.

77
00:06:23,166 --> 00:06:28,651
Expediting: 300 more and the remaining downtime is halved,

78
00:06:28,671 --> 00:06:33,586
yet it's still a day late; the next slide shows why.

79
00:06:33,986 --> 00:06:45,657
Outsourcing: one unit's three shafts cost 43.94, plus 50 freight per batch;

80
00:06:45,677 --> 00:06:50,646
we need 4 units, 225.76 dollars.

81
00:06:52,166 --> 00:06:54,346
The supervisor wanted to expedite.

82
00:06:54,746 --> 00:06:58,832
The remaining 24 hours are halved to 12,

83
00:06:58,852 --> 00:07:03,554
and the grinder is back at 10 on Monday night.

84
00:07:03,954 --> 00:07:11,020
But of the 12 hours saved, 10 are at night, when the shop doesn't work anyway;

85
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:16,107
the only extra production is 2 hours on Tuesday morning.

86
00:07:16,507 --> 00:07:22,701
L drops from 9 to 7, still 12 minutes short. 300 dollars spent,

87
00:07:22,721 --> 00:07:27,927
the robot order still a day late, 500 dollars in all.

88
00:07:28,327 --> 00:07:31,752
A faster repair doesn't mean an on-time delivery.

89
00:07:33,272 --> 00:07:38,024
Which is cheaper depends on the gap.

90
00:07:38,424 --> 00:07:46,163
An hour of outsourced grinding costs 67.60 dollars, less than 78 of overtime,

91
00:07:46,183 --> 00:07:49,589
but each batch adds 50 of freight.

92
00:07:49,989 --> 00:08:00,929
They cost the same when 78 h equals 67.6 h plus 50, at about 4.8 hours.

93
00:08:01,329 --> 00:08:06,069
A smaller gap favours overtime, a bigger one outsourcing.

94
00:08:06,469 --> 00:08:18,845
Today it's only 2.2 hours, so overtime at 171.60 beats outsourcing at 225.76.

95
00:08:20,365 --> 00:08:25,539
The hand calculation quietly assumes there's enough overtime,

96
00:08:25,559 --> 00:08:32,265
that outsourced parts return on time and that the orders ahead aren't affected.

97
00:08:32,665 --> 00:08:38,959
So the hub also simulates: it snapshots the plant, makes a fast-forward copy,

98
00:08:38,979 --> 00:08:44,863
applies one option and runs minute by minute on the real shift calendar,

99
00:08:44,883 --> 00:08:47,405
without touching the real shop.

100
00:08:47,805 --> 00:08:51,564
This is the Gantt chart of Virtual lab 3-2,

101
00:08:51,584 --> 00:08:57,012
with the play-head sweeping through the week: red is the stop,

102
00:08:57,032 --> 00:08:59,385
yellow frames are overtime.

103
00:08:59,785 --> 00:09:05,867
With overtime the robot order is done at Wed 17:12, on time;

104
00:09:05,887 --> 00:09:13,190
outsourcing is on time too; expediting and doing nothing are a day late.

105
00:09:13,590 --> 00:09:18,010
Delivery first, then money: overtime ranks first.

106
00:09:19,530 --> 00:09:27,043
On screen the downtime moves from 8 to 48 hours and the four options' costs

107
00:09:27,063 --> 00:09:28,550
change with it.

108
00:09:28,950 --> 00:09:35,976
From 7 to 22 hours the repair ends at night and L stays at 7 hours;

109
00:09:35,996 --> 00:09:41,865
doing nothing costs 200 dollars all the way to 25 hours.

110
00:09:42,265 --> 00:09:50,655
From 26 hours Summit's slack of 10.8 regular hours runs out and it is late too.

111
00:09:51,055 --> 00:09:57,861
At 30 hours the hand calculation says the robot order is 8.2 hours short,

112
00:09:57,881 --> 00:10:01,415
so 639.60 dollars of overtime will do;

113
00:10:01,435 --> 00:10:07,119
the simulation says the grinder is back only at 4 pm Tuesday,

114
00:10:07,139 --> 00:10:12,075
and even 4 hours a day leaves both orders a day late.

115
00:10:12,475 --> 00:10:16,628
The hub's ranking then recommends doing nothing,

116
00:10:16,648 --> 00:10:21,671
400 dollars with two late orders; expediting, 500 dollars,

117
00:10:21,691 --> 00:10:27,496
saves Summit and leaves only the robot order late, yet ranks lower,

118
00:10:27,516 --> 00:10:33,495
because the rule looks at the latest order, not at how many are late.

119
00:10:33,895 --> 00:10:38,434
From 31 hours expediting ranks first.

120
00:10:38,834 --> 00:10:43,508
Which order to save is for people to decide;

121
00:10:43,528 --> 00:10:48,736
Lab 15's discussion question 2 asks exactly this.

122
00:10:50,256 --> 00:10:51,996
Decide and act.

123
00:10:53,516 --> 00:10:55,288
Don't compare on money alone.

124
00:10:55,688 --> 00:11:03,813
Doing nothing costs only 200 dollars, less than outsourcing; why not choose it?

125
00:11:04,213 --> 00:11:07,336
Because delivery comes before money:

126
00:11:07,356 --> 00:11:11,963
a day late may stop the robot plant's line for a day,

127
00:11:11,983 --> 00:11:16,677
and the penalty is only what a contract can spell out.

128
00:11:17,077 --> 00:11:22,344
Lay out the risks too: overtime needs a skilled grinder operator,

129
00:11:22,364 --> 00:11:26,167
and the grinder is already overdue for service,

130
00:11:26,187 --> 00:11:31,128
so as we saw in Lecture 6 extra hours raise the failure risk;

131
00:11:31,148 --> 00:11:34,219
outsourced parts need full inspection,

132
00:11:34,239 --> 00:11:38,855
and if the other shop is a day late it's all for nothing.

133
00:11:39,255 --> 00:11:42,853
Option C, expediting, isn't shown:

134
00:11:42,873 --> 00:11:49,555
at 500 dollars it is still a day late and wins on no dimension.

135
00:11:49,955 --> 00:11:55,695
On all three dimensions: overtime first, outsourcing as the fallback.

136
00:11:57,215 --> 00:12:00,815
Finally, do it yourself in the digital factory.

137
00:12:01,215 --> 00:12:05,675
Lab 15 uses comparison mode.

138
00:12:06,075 --> 00:12:09,299
The instructor injects a breakdown,

139
00:12:09,319 --> 00:12:14,860
and without opening the hub you trace it from the home page,

140
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:19,865
shop terminals and order pages: which machine stopped,

141
00:12:19,885 --> 00:12:27,095
which work orders are queued, which orders they belong to, how many days late.

142
00:12:27,495 --> 00:12:31,755
The platform scores you against the system's chain.

143
00:12:32,155 --> 00:12:38,470
Then try at least three options in the what-if workbench and save them;

144
00:12:38,490 --> 00:12:43,110
choose one, give reasons on delivery, cost and risk,

145
00:12:43,130 --> 00:12:46,233
and only then see the hub's advice.

146
00:12:46,633 --> 00:12:52,919
The option becomes an action that runs after the instructor approves it;

147
00:12:52,939 --> 00:12:59,751
when the work orders are done the platform reviews prediction against reality.

148
00:13:00,151 --> 00:13:04,636
Note that the lab tracks only the output shaft,

149
00:13:04,656 --> 00:13:10,771
so its numbers are smaller than today's; the method is the same.

150
00:13:12,291 --> 00:13:15,191
A causal chain doesn't stop at the factory gate.

151
00:13:15,591 --> 00:13:23,683
Each of the robot plant's collaborative robots uses WQR-105 units in the two

152
00:13:23,703 --> 00:13:25,391
big base joints,

153
00:13:25,411 --> 00:13:31,902
so 20 reducers make 10 robots; these are illustrative values.

154
00:13:32,302 --> 00:13:38,340
If we're a day late, its line builds 10 fewer, and its customers wait too.

155
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:41,328
Robots do the same thing inside:

156
00:13:41,348 --> 00:13:47,603
when a controller reports overcurrent on joint 3, it traces up for the cause,

157
00:13:47,623 --> 00:13:53,144
an overload, a worn reducer or a collision, and down for the impact:

158
00:13:53,164 --> 00:13:58,686
this robot stops, the line's cycle time drops, today's output falls.

159
00:13:59,086 --> 00:14:02,157
That's robot fault diagnosis.

160
00:14:03,677 --> 00:14:06,097
Daily life works the same way.

161
00:14:06,497 --> 00:14:12,987
A takeaway: the restaurant accepts it in 2 minutes, cooks for 12,

162
00:14:13,007 --> 00:14:19,797
a rider is found in 5, the ride takes 11; the promise is 30 minutes.

163
00:14:20,197 --> 00:14:27,259
At the lunch peak the kitchen takes 25 minutes, and the food arrives after 43.

164
00:14:27,659 --> 00:14:32,239
The rider arrives early and just waits for the food.

165
00:14:32,639 --> 00:14:39,050
The chain has no slack, so the kitchen's extra 13 minutes all become lateness.

166
00:14:39,450 --> 00:14:44,348
Calling the rider won't help: the kitchen is where it's stuck,

167
00:14:44,368 --> 00:14:46,965
and the rider is only downstream.

168
00:14:47,365 --> 00:14:50,355
When the app updates its arrival time,

169
00:14:50,375 --> 00:14:55,345
it's running a causal chain and a simulation in the background.

170
00:14:56,865 --> 00:14:58,064
Back to the start.

171
00:14:58,464 --> 00:15:04,886
The grinder is down 24 hours, but only 9 production hours are really lost.

172
00:15:05,286 --> 00:15:10,494
The robot order had 6.8 hours of slack and is 2.2 hours short:

173
00:15:10,514 --> 00:15:15,554
doing nothing makes it a day late with a 200-dollar penalty,

174
00:15:15,574 --> 00:15:18,674
while Summit and Harbor stay on time.

175
00:15:19,074 --> 00:15:25,549
The supervisor's three days and 600 dollars are three times the real figure,

176
00:15:25,569 --> 00:15:30,934
and the expediting he wanted costs 300 without saving the date.

177
00:15:31,334 --> 00:15:34,633
Recommendation: after the repair,

178
00:15:34,653 --> 00:15:41,071
2 hours of overtime on Tuesday and 12 minutes more on Wednesday,

179
00:15:41,091 --> 00:15:46,705
171.60 dollars, and the order ships on time on Thursday.

180
00:15:47,105 --> 00:15:50,650
The fallback is outsourcing 4 units.

181
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:56,275
Overtime needs the plant manager's approval before the shift calendar changes.

182
00:15:57,795 --> 00:16:05,104
Here's Animation 3-1, which you can try yourself on the WenQuest platform.

183
00:16:05,124 --> 00:16:12,135
The numbers in the results panel are blurred; reading them is your job.

184
00:16:12,535 --> 00:16:19,686
First press Chapter, 24 hours, then drag the downtime from zero to 48 hours.

185
00:16:19,706 --> 00:16:22,328
Which order turns red first,

186
00:16:22,348 --> 00:16:27,801
and why does almost nothing change between 7 and 22 hours?

187
00:16:28,201 --> 00:16:34,779
Next press Friday 3 pm, 20 hours. With the same 20-hour stop,

188
00:16:34,799 --> 00:16:41,701
how does the production time lost compare with a stop on Monday?

189
00:16:42,101 --> 00:16:49,539
Then move the robot order to the front. Which order turns red now?

190
00:16:49,559 --> 00:16:53,041
Do the penalties go up or down?

191
00:16:53,441 --> 00:16:59,163
Finally press Replay and watch the chain light up from machine to customer.

192
00:16:59,183 --> 00:17:02,301
Ask where each layer's numbers come from.

193
00:17:03,821 --> 00:17:10,709
Virtual lab 3-2: overtime, expediting or outsourcing? Press Chapter, 24 hours,

194
00:17:10,729 --> 00:17:16,643
and check the four options' costs and deliveries against Table 3-3.

195
00:17:17,043 --> 00:17:23,354
Drag the downtime from 2 to 48 hours and see where the recommendation changes.

196
00:17:23,754 --> 00:17:28,859
Press 30 hours. Work out the overtime cost by hand,

197
00:17:28,879 --> 00:17:37,100
then raise the daily overtime cap to 240 minutes and see what the simulation says.

198
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:42,114
Then press Expedite 6 hours late: does it do any worse?

199
00:17:42,134 --> 00:17:48,939
Finally play the Gantt chart and watch the grinder's week under the four options.

200
00:17:48,959 --> 00:17:52,984
I won't give the answers; they're yours to find.

201
00:17:53,384 --> 00:17:58,244
Both labs come with a guide and a report template on the platform.

202
00:17:59,764 --> 00:18:04,354
After class you'll do Lab 15 in the WenQuest digital factory.

203
00:18:04,374 --> 00:18:07,000
The instructor injects a breakdown,

204
00:18:07,020 --> 00:18:12,517
and without opening the hub you trace it yourself: which machine stopped,

205
00:18:12,537 --> 00:18:18,412
which work orders are queued, which orders they belong to, how many days late.

206
00:18:18,812 --> 00:18:23,684
Then try at least three options in the what-if workbench,

207
00:18:23,704 --> 00:18:29,778
choose one and explain it on delivery, cost and risk. Until you submit,

208
00:18:29,798 --> 00:18:32,352
the hub's advice stays hidden.

209
00:18:32,752 --> 00:18:38,991
Your option becomes an action that runs only after the instructor approves it;

210
00:18:39,011 --> 00:18:41,398
when the work orders are done,

211
00:18:41,418 --> 00:18:46,292
the platform reviews how far the simulation was from reality.

212
00:18:47,812 --> 00:18:51,678
The assignment has three tasks. One, calculation:

213
00:18:51,698 --> 00:18:57,228
how much production time does a 20-hour stop on Friday afternoon lose;

214
00:18:57,248 --> 00:19:02,938
for a 30-hour stop on Monday, work out overtime and outsourcing by hand,

215
00:19:02,958 --> 00:19:06,427
then simulate it and explain the difference.

216
00:19:06,827 --> 00:19:12,444
Two, analysis: move the robot order to the front; how late is each order,

217
00:19:12,464 --> 00:19:18,468
what are the penalties, and what do you recommend on money, delivery and risk?

218
00:19:18,868 --> 00:19:21,264
Three, the Lab 15 report,

219
00:19:21,284 --> 00:19:28,416
focusing on your causal chain against the system's and your choice against

220
00:19:28,436 --> 00:19:30,058
the hub's advice.

221
00:19:31,578 --> 00:19:35,767
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example:

222
00:19:35,787 --> 00:19:39,518
a 24-hour stop from 10 on Monday morning,

223
00:19:39,538 --> 00:19:43,360
how many production hours are really lost?

224
00:19:43,760 --> 00:19:48,051
If expediting halves the remaining downtime,

225
00:19:48,071 --> 00:19:53,340
do you always gain half of it back as production time?

226
00:19:53,740 --> 00:19:59,461
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

227
00:20:00,981 --> 00:20:03,121
Three things to remember.

228
00:20:03,521 --> 00:20:07,072
One: rules find exceptions in the data,

229
00:20:07,092 --> 00:20:13,023
and a causal chain traces up to the cause and down to the impact,

230
00:20:13,043 --> 00:20:15,679
with evidence for every link.

231
00:20:16,079 --> 00:20:19,005
Two: impact must be calculated;

232
00:20:19,025 --> 00:20:25,659
the production time really lost is the overlap of downtime and shifts,

233
00:20:25,679 --> 00:20:32,979
an order is late only beyond its slack, and affected is not the same as late.

234
00:20:33,379 --> 00:20:40,057
Three: simulate the options; fast-forward overtime, expediting and outsourcing,

235
00:20:40,077 --> 00:20:45,399
rank by delivery first and money second, and lay out the risks.

